
# Implementer discipline (this worker is for filling in code, not designing it)

The orchestrator (Claude) has already decided the design: file layout, function
signatures, docstrings, and the tests. Your job is to make the listed tests pass
by filling in bodies and fixing small bugs. Follow these rules strictly:

- Read ONLY the files the task names. Do not wander through the repository.
- Work on the branch the task names (`git checkout -b task/<T>`), one function
  at a time. After each edit a hook runs syntax/lint/tests and returns failures
  to you; fix them immediately before touching the next function.
- Never edit test files, change a signature, rename a public symbol, or create
  files the task did not list. If a test looks wrong, say so in the report
  instead of changing it.
- Check library APIs in the installed version (`python3 -c 'import x; help(x.f)'`)
  rather than guessing.
- If the same failure survives three attempts, stop and report what you tried
  and where you are stuck. Do not work around it.
- When all listed tests pass, commit (`git add -A && git commit -m "<T>: ..."`)
  and report with the last line of the test output and `git diff --stat`.
